  <h1>India’s Energy Transition — Balancing Coal and Renewables</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India’s energy policy in 2024 reflected a delicate balancing act between coal dependence and renewable expansion. While coal continues to provide ~70% of electricity generation, India has also set ambitious renewable targets: 500 GW of non-fossil fuel capacity by 2030 and net-zero by 2070. The Hindu noted that India’s transition is shaped by energy security needs, climate commitments, and developmental imperatives.
________________________________________
India’s Current Energy Mix
•	Coal: Still the backbone of India’s energy, ensuring base load power.
•	Renewables: Solar (70 GW+), wind, hydro, and biomass growing rapidly.
•	Nuclear: Small but strategic share (~7 GW).
•	Oil & Gas: Heavy reliance on imports (85% of crude oil).
________________________________________
Drivers of Renewable Push
1.	Climate Commitments: COP26 pledge — 50% energy from non-fossil sources by 2030.
2.	Global Leadership: International Solar Alliance (ISA) and LiFE (Lifestyle for Environment) initiative.
3.	Domestic Needs: Reduce import dependence and pollution.
4.	Technological Change: Falling solar tariffs, EV adoption, and battery storage.
________________________________________
Challenges in Transition
•	Coal Lock-in: Coal sector employs millions and powers industries; sudden exit risks economic shocks.
•	Financial Stress: DISCOMs’ losses hinder renewable integration.
•	Intermittency Issues: Solar/wind need storage solutions for reliability.
•	Infrastructure: Grid modernization and transmission capacity lagging.
•	Political Economy: Coal-producing states resist rapid phase-outs.
________________________________________
Recent Developments (2024)
•	Solar Parks Expansion: Rajasthan, Gujarat, and Tamil Nadu leading projects.
•	Green Hydrogen Mission: Initial pilot projects launched with subsidies.
•	Coal Expansion: Despite green push, India approved new coal mines to ensure energy security amid demand surge.
•	International Cooperation: Partnership with EU, Japan, and US on clean energy financing.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-III (Economy & Environment): Energy security, climate change, renewables.
•	GS-II (IR): Climate diplomacy and international cooperation.
•	Essay:
o	“Energy security vs environmental sustainability: A governance dilemma.”
o	“Coal and solar — India’s twin pillars of development.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Development vs sustainability debates in global governance.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Phased Transition: Gradual coal reduction with just transition for workers.<br/>2.	Storage Technologies: Invest in batteries, pumped hydro.<br/>3.	Green Finance: Expand sovereign green bonds and PPPs.<br/>4.	Grid Upgradation: Smart grids for renewable integration.<br/>5.	Behavioral Change: Promote energy efficiency under LiFE initiative.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s energy transition is neither linear nor simple — it requires balancing economic growth, social equity, and climate obligations. Coal remains indispensable in the short term, but the trajectory toward renewables is irreversible. For UPSC aspirants, this issue exemplifies how policy trade-offs define governance in emerging economies.</p>
